  "final_span": "ten hours on the Qumashan neighbourhood of the city, close to the centre of Tripoli.\nWitnesses also report that more than a dozen airstrikes struck sites near Tripoli last night.\nAs of now, there is still no clear indication of which targets may have been targeted by these bombings.\nThe neighborhoods reportedly affected, as shown in videos from various local news sites like Yamaniya TV, are situated on the outskirts of the city and not near its center.\nOn Monday, reports in Libyan media indicated that NATO jets conducted around 50 strikes on airport vicinity targets in the country.\nThe peacekeeping force of the Security Council, UNSCO, reported that three civilians from one family were killed on Sunday in Zuwarah, a western town of Libya, when a shell struck the hospital they were in.\nUNSCO has deeply lamented the incident.\nUNSCO Co-Ordinator Ingram has warned in a press release that \"the international community cannot stand idly by while Libyans kill, torture, rape and abduct civilians in a brutal crackdown on all pro-democracy demonstrations.\"\nIn another statement, Patrick, echoed the urgent calls for a humanitarian pause in fighting, both to ensure compliance with the UN Security Council resolution 1970, and enable humanitarian aid to reach people in Libya: \"I call upon the international community to provide the resources needed to enable humanitarian actors to provide assistance especially in areas around Benghazi, Misrata, Zlitan, and the southern half of the country.\"\nAccording to a press statement from the Special Representative of the Secretary General and the Head of the UN Mission, Ian Martin, Tripoli had \"an unprecedented number of fuel tankers\" in its streets of its capital over the weekend, apparently due to the fuel being needed to fuel Libyan army planes at the Safe military airbase north of the city, its primary target destination.\n\"Some fuel tankers have been hit and are burning in the streets of Tripoli,\" Martin is reported to have said, \" apresenting a real danger that these attacks could spark a wider conflagration with an unimagin",
